strategy=th['strategy'].replace('[rho^{-1}partial_theta, partial_rho] = -(1/rho^2)partial_theta = -(1/rho)n','[rho^{-1}partial_theta, partial_rho] = +(1/rho^2)partial_theta = +(1/rho)n')
a=strategy.index('(c) Compute');b=strategy.index('(d) The displayed')
strategy=strategy[:a]+'''(c) K is future timelike exactly on the stated U_Omega and is Killing by direct coordinate differentiation. Projecting the symmetric derivative of n=gamma K to its rest space kills the derivative-of-gamma terms (K is parallel to n) and the Killing terms, so theta=0. Put kappa=eta(K,.)=-dx^0+(Omega/c)(x^1 dx^2-x^2 dx^1). Then d kappa=2(Omega/c)dx^1 wedge dx^2 and kappa wedge d kappa=-2(Omega/c)dx^0 wedge dx^1 wedge dx^2, nonzero exactly when Omega!=0. Since n-flat=gamma kappa, n-flat wedge d(n-flat)=gamma^2 kappa wedge d kappa; the stated ambient Frobenius/vorticity result follows, including at the axis by these smooth Cartesian formulas. The Omega=0 limit has the constant unit field. On the ring cylinder, substitute x^0=ct, x^1=Rcos(varphi+Omega t), x^2=Rsin(varphi+Omega t) to obtain the displayed metric and covector. Its one-dimensional kernel is the line field generated by partial_varphi+[Omega R^2/(c^2-Omega^2R^2)]partial_t, whose integral gives the signed holonomy. A globally defined time-oriented rest-synchronization function would have differential a positive multiple of dt-[Omega R^2/(c^2-Omega^2R^2)]dvarphi. Its integral around a fixed-t ring would have nonzero constant sign for Omega!=0, contradicting exactness of that differential. No local nonintegrability is attributed to the cylinder's one-dimensional rest-line field. '''+strategy[b:]
a=strategy.index('(d) The displayed')
strategy=strategy[:a]+'''(d) Write beta=q_1'/c, beta'=q_1''/c, x^0=ct and n_b=(-gamma,gamma beta,0,0), so partial_0=(1/c)d/dt and partial_1 n_b=0. Differentiation gives partial_0 n_0=-gamma^3 beta beta'/c and partial_0 n_1=gamma^3 beta'/c, using 1+gamma^2 beta^2=gamma^2. Contract with s=(gamma beta,gamma,0,0): s^0[s^0 partial_0 n_0+s^1 partial_0 n_1]=gamma^5 beta beta'(1-beta^2)/c=gamma^3 beta beta'/c=gamma^3 q_1' q_1''/c^3. Projection to the rest space does not change this contraction since s is rest-orthogonal. All transverse expansion components vanish, so it is nonzero exactly under the stated condition, and fixed chart-time separations remain xi_1-xi_2. The material fracture conclusion is excluded. All computations use finite algebra and the declared calculus, with no choice principle.'''
